  <div class="prose">
    <h2 id="lookup">Naming one entry</h2>
    <p>
      An entry is a model at a provider. Its <code>ref</code> is <code>provider/model</code>. Provider ids never contain a slash, so the provider is everything before the first slash and the
      model id is the rest, slashes included: <code>openrouter/anthropic/claude-opus-5.5</code> is model <code>anthropic/claude-opus-5.5</code> at provider <code>openrouter</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Because model ids contain slashes (and sometimes <code>:</code>, <code>@</code> or <code>~</code>), the lookup takes the name in the query string, not the path. Either form works; URL-encode
      the values:
    </p>
    <pre>/api/v1/entry?ref=openrouter%2Fanthropic%2Fclaude-opus-5.5
/api/v1/entry?provider=openrouter&model=anthropic%2Fclaude-opus-5.5</pre>

    <h2 id="filters">Filters for <code>/api/v1/models</code></h2>
    <p>
      Filters combine with <em>and</em>. A parameter that is not in this list, a value that is not allowed, or the same parameter given twice is answered with <code>400</code> and a list of
      what is wrong. The catalog page of this site uses the same parameters in its own address, so any view there can be copied as an API request.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table class="facts">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Parameter</th><th scope="col">Value</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          PARAMS.map((param) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row"><code>{param.name}</code></th>
              <td>{param.values ? param.values.join(param.kind === "list" ? ", " : " | ") : param.kind === "integer" ? "whole number" : param.kind === "boolean" ? "true | false" : param.kind === "list" ? "comma-separated" : "text"}</td>
              <td>{param.description}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2 id="paging">Paging and order</h2>
    <p>
      <code>limit</code> is 1 to {MAX_LIMIT} (default {DEFAULT_LIMIT}); <code>offset</code> skips entries. The answer holds <code>total</code> and <code>next_offset</code>, which is
      <code>null</code> on the last page. Order is fixed: the sort key, then provider id, then model id, so paging never repeats or skips an entry within one dataset version. Entries with no
      value for the sort key come last in both directions.
    </p>

    <h2 id="unknown">Unknown, zero and unsupported</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>A limit that is <strong>absent</strong> is unknown. It is never written as 0, and it never matches <code>min_context</code> or <code>min_output</code>.</li>
      <li><code>cache: "no"</code> means the source says the entry does not cache. <code>cache: "unknown"</code> means the source says nothing.</li>
      <li><code>max_output</code> is a maximum. A provider's default output setting is a different thing and is not published as this field.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="caching">Versions, freshness and conditional requests</h2>
    <p>
      Every answer carries <code>X-Dataset-Version</code> (a hash of the published data; it changes only when the data does), <code>X-Dataset-Generated-At</code> and
      <code>X-Upstream-Fetched-At</code>. The <code>ETag</code> is the dataset version. Send it back as <code>If-None-Match</code> and you get <code>304</code> with no body until the data
      changes. Answers may be cached for five minutes. Enveloped answers repeat the three values under <code>meta</code>; <code>/api.json</code> has no envelope, so there they are in the headers
      only.
    </p>
    <p>
      There are two version numbers and they are independent. <strong><code>v1</code></strong> in the path is the version of this API: its endpoints, parameters and envelope.
      <strong>{meta.upstream_schema}</strong> is the schema of the model fields, which models.dev still calls experimental and may change; <code>/api/v1/meta</code> reports it as
      <code>upstream_schema</code>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="errors">Errors</h2>
    <p>Every error has the same shape, with the matching HTTP status (<code>400</code>, <code>404</code>, <code>405</code>, <code>503</code>):</p>
    <pre>{`{ "error": { "code": "invalid_parameters", "message": "One or more query parameters are not valid.",
             "details": [ { "param": "min_context", "message": "Must be a whole number, 0 or more." } ] } }`}</pre>

    <h2 id="extension">The <code>providers_dev</code> extension</h2>
    <p>
      Provider and model objects keep the models.dev v2 fields with their upstream meaning. Everything providers.dev adds is under one key, <code>providers_dev</code>, so it cannot be
      mistaken for upstream data.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table class="facts">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Field</th><th scope="col">On</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>origin</code></th><td>both</td><td><code>upstream</code>: imported from models.dev. <code>added</code>: models.dev has no such provider or entry.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>access</code></th><td>both</td><td><code>api</code>, <code>subscription</code> or <code>unknown</code>. A classification only.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>access_basis</code>, <code>access_reviewed</code>, <code>access_source</code></th><td>provider</td><td>Why the provider is classified that way, and whether we reviewed it or it follows from the default or the provider's name.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>provenance[]</code></th><td>both</td><td>One item per corrected field, where providers.dev replaced, hid or removed an upstream value: <code>path</code>, <code>op</code> (<code>set</code>, <code>unknown</code>, <code>delete</code>), the <code>upstream</code> value, our <code>value</code>, the <code>reason</code> and the <code>evidence</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>cache.observed[]</code></th><td>model</td><td>Cache evaluations on this entry: access route, client, options, date, warm checks by age, and the three summary ages (<code>all_warm_through_s</code>, <code>last_warm_s</code>, <code>first_all_cold_s</code>). Never merged into the documented <code>capabilities.cache</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>limits.measured[]</code></th><td>model</td><td>Limits measured on this entry: context, output, and the largest input accepted or refused. On an upstream entry they sit next to the documented <code>limit</code> and replace nothing.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>limits.from_measurement[]</code></th><td>model</td><td>On an added entry, where there is no documented limit: which of <code>limit.context</code> and <code>limit.output</code> are measured values.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>inherited</code></th><td>model</td><td>For an added entry: which upstream entry its name, family and dates were taken from. Limits, caching and prices are never inherited.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>evidence[]</code></th><td>both</td><td>Sources for an added entry or provider: <code>kind</code> (<code>documented</code>, <code>measured</code>, <code>listed</code>), <code>url</code>, <code>date</code>, <code>note</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>verified</code></th><td>model</td><td>The newest date among the providers.dev evidence for the entry. Imported entries have none: imported is not verified.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2 id="compat">How compatible <code>/api.json</code> is with models.dev</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The root is an object keyed by provider id; each provider has <code>models</code> keyed by model id. Provider ids, model ids, <code>canonical_id</code> and field meanings are upstream's.</li>
      <li>Every provider and model has one extra key, <code>providers_dev</code>. A strict v2 parser must allow or drop it.</li>
      <li>Entries with <code>origin: "added"</code> hold only values with evidence. They can lack fields that models.dev requires (<code>modalities</code>, <code>capabilities</code>, <code>limit</code>, <code>cost</code>, <code>api</code>). Skip them, or treat missing as unknown.</li>
      <li>Fields listed in <code>provenance</code> differ from upstream on purpose.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>So it is a superset in the same shape, not a byte-compatible mirror.</p>

    <h2 id="examples">Examples</h2>
    <h3>curl</h3>
    <pre>{curl}</pre>
    <h3>TypeScript</h3>
